New York, New York
April 29, 2025
Statement at the UNGA the resolution “Global Health and Foreign Policy: Rethinking Health Promotion as a Transformative Path Towards Improved and More Sustainable Well-Being for All.”
AS DELIVERED
Mr. President, the United States called for a vote on this resolution due to several provisions which run counter to U.S. policies.
We greatly appreciate the efforts by Thailand and the other co-facilitators to further integrate health promotion into foreign policy toward improved global health. This text, however, is overly broad and touches on multiple policy areas beyond the scope of the resolution, such as intellectual property, financing, and climate change.
UN agencies must respect the mandates of other processes and institutions. While the UN and WTO share some common interests, they have different roles, rules, and memberships. The UN is not the appropriate venue for discussions about the TRIPS Agreement. The flexibilities provided for in the TRIPS Agreement are for use at the discretion of WTO Members as they best see fit. It is inappropriate for the UN to mandate or influence when and how WTO Members act. Selectively calling out and characterizing the TRIPS Agreement suggests that the UN is attempting to influence issues within the independent mandate of the WTO.
Additionally, the United States requires all references to technology transfer include the modifier “voluntary” and “on mutually agreed terms.” Any sharing of proprietary information, expertise, or technology needs to be both voluntary and on mutually agreed terms. We firmly underscore the importance of regulatory and legal environments that support innovation.
References to global health financing are also problematic. The United States does not support calls for additional funding or the proliferation of new financing
mechanisms. Member States should instead focus on domestic resource mobilization.
The United States cannot accept an unnecessary and distracting focus on climate in this body’s work. In lieu of general references to climate change as a policy matter or root cause of threats, the United States encourages Member States to focus on specific environmental threats, such as threats to clean air and clean water, wildfires, floods,”
“drought, sea-level rise, and sensible environmental protections, such as increasing access to affordable, reliable, and secure energy, enhancing resilience, and disaster planning.
The text also includes references that do not appropriately focus on the needs and perspectives of women and girls, including “gender-sensitive.” It is the policy of the United States to use clear and accurate language that recognizes women are biologically female, and men are biologically male. It is important to acknowledge the biological reality of sex to support the needs and perspectives of women and girls.
The United States will not accept references to sexual and reproductive health care services or derivatives of this term, especially when such references are not caveated to respect national policies and priorities. There is no international human right to abortion.
The text also includes references to “health equity.” This term lacks definitional consensus. The United States objects to the inclusion of this term.
More broadly, the resolution references several non-binding documents, many of which do not add value to the resolution, or are more appropriately “noted,” rather than “affirmed” or “welcomed.” To that point, the United States has been clear that we will no longer reaffirm the 2030 Agenda for Sustainable Development or the Sustainable Development Goals as a matter of course. They advance an unacceptable program of soft global governance and undercut state sovereignty and the ownership of and responsibility for their own development.
Finally, the United States rejects the call for a WHO-moderated pandemic treaty. An organization which cannot even admit the source of the worst pandemic in decades is ill- equipped to shepherd such an agreement.
