Statement on the Modalities, format and organization of the high-level meeting of the General Assembly on the appraisal of the United Nations Global Plan of Action to Combat Trafficking in Persons” Resolution
AS DELIVERED
The United States welcomes the adoption of this resolution. We commend the leadership for their facilitation of the text.
The United States is firmly committed to combating human trafficking. Human trafficking tears apart communities, fuels criminal activity, and threatens the national security of the United States and other countries. The international community must work together to confront this threat, support victims and survivors, and hold traffickers accountable for their heinous crimes.
Combating trafficking in persons makes our country—and the world—safer, stronger, and more prosperous, a sentiment that is echoed in the Global Plan of Action to Combat Trafficking in Persons, which notes that trafficking in persons “has negative effects on development, peace and security, … and human rights.” The United States supports the Global Plan of Action to Combat Trafficking in Persons framework of preventing trafficking in persons, protecting victims and survivors, prosecuting traffickers, and strengthening anti-trafficking partnerships—a framework that reflects our Trafficking Victims Protection Act.
A concern we have about this resolution is its reaffirmation of Agenda 2030 and the Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs) in PP5. Although framed in neutral language, Agenda 2030 and the SDGs advance a program of soft global governance that is inconsistent with U.S. sovereignty and adverse to the rights and interests of Americans.
Lastly, the United States disassociates from OP9 of the text due to the language on gender parity. The United States strongly supports the protection of women and girls, the defense of their rights, and their empowerment. However, the United States opposes the use of quotas to achieve parity for women and girls. We regret that clearer language on women’s and men’s meaningful participation in the high-level conference was not incorporated into the text.
