Jonathan Shrier
Acting Representative
New York, New York
April 29, 2025
AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Mr. President. The United States extends its gratitude to Mr. Robert Petit and the International Impartial and Independent Mechanism for their critical work to advance justice and accountability in Syria.
Stability in Syria requires holding perpetrators of crimes over the past 14 years accountable and providing meaningful justice to victims.
Even without access to the country, over the past eight years the IIIM has made remarkable strides in collecting, consolidating, preserving, and analyzing evidence of international humanitarian law violations and human rights violations and abuses.
This work has supported landmark trials convicting and indicting regime officials and ISIS members of war crimes.
With Assad out of Syria, there is now a historic opportunity for the IIIM to expand on these strides and more comprehensively fulfill its mandate.
Mr. President, we welcomed the interim authorities providing access for the IIIM to enter Syria for the first time. Continued access is imperative for the urgent documentation and preservation of evidence of regime abuses to support ongoing and future accountability efforts.
We urge interim authorities to provide the IIIM continued and unrestricted access to the country and support its mandate.
Accountability in Syria requires a collective effort, and we encourage the interim authorities, UN entities, and Syrian civil society organizations to effectively coordinate.
Mr. President, the United States supports the continued work of the IIIM and urges member states to fund the IIIM as it receives increased demands for assistance during this window of opportunity to shape a “new” Syria.
Meaningful justice and accountability are critical to prevent atrocities – like those we saw on the coast – and build sustainable peace in this country marred by destabilizing violence. Now is the time more than ever to reaffirm the IIIM’s mandate and support this critical work.
Thank you.
