AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Chair.
The United States supports the rights of Indigenous Peoples.
Unfortunately, the rights of certain Indigenous populations, including those of Tibetans and Uyghurs in China, are not being respected.
The Chinese Communist Party’s efforts to erase Tibetan identity, militarize the Himalayas, and co-opt Tibetan Buddhism threaten the human rights of six million Tibetans in China and many tens of thousands in the Tibetan diaspora.
Additionally, China, under the direction and control of the Chinese Communist Party, has committed genocide and crimes against humanity targeting predominantly Muslim Uyghurs and members of other ethnic and religious minority groups in Xinjiang.
The United States urges China to respect the human rights and fundamental freedoms of Tibetans, including the ability to express and practice their religion without interference or coercion, and preserve their culture and identity.
Likewise, we urge respect for the human rights and fundamental freedoms of Uyghurs, who are subject to so-called reeducation, arbitrary detention, torture, sexual violence, and forced labor in China. We urge all governments in countries where Uyghurs seek protection not to forcibly return ethnic Uyghurs to China.
We will continue to champion the rights and dignity of Uyghurs and Tibetans, and stand against the Chinese Communist Party’s abuses, both at home and abroad.
I thank you.
