Jonathan Shrier
Acting U.S. Representative to the UN Economic and Social Council
New York, New York
April 24, 2025
U.S. Statement on the Special Meeting of ECOSOC on Forced Displacement and Refugee Protection
AS PREPARED
Thank you Mr. President and panelists for this briefing on best practices.
The international community, as has been reflected in this panel, has struggled to respond to the scale and complexity of refugee needs.
We must recognize that the issue of protracted displacement requires a fundamental re-thinking, one that centers true respect for core principles of national sovereignty, border security, and the rule of law in our response.
We must also maintain focus on the first priority, which should be to incentivize world leaders to end conflicts that lead to cross-border displacement. We will discuss this at greater length in the Security Council next week. We also underscore that every person who feels forced to flee his or her homeland represents a failure of political leadership.
We should recognize that, for too long, there has been an over-reliance on funding and other support from the United States. To meet current needs, there must be more robust burden-sharing around the world. Other donors must step up now because American taxpayers will not continue to inequitably shoulder this outsized burden.
But more funding alone is not enough—and, in fact, in some cases it may serve as a moral hazard that prolongs refugee displacement. We believe protection for refugees should not be managed through long-term encampment practices. The refugee protection system was never intended to suspend refugees in permanent limbo like this. We should not continue to subsidize this practice.
Furthermore, we recognize that, in certain contexts, fostering refugee self-reliance and economic opportunity—in line with local economic conditions and labor needs—can uphold the dignity of refugees while reducing the humanitarian burden on donor countries. In other contexts, as a matter of national sovereignty and rule of law, reasonable restrictions on work for refugees and migrants may be necessary. When refugees remain close to home and are integrated economically, they can more seamlessly return when conditions allow.
We recognize countries that are moving in this direction, including Uganda, Kenya, Colombia, Zambia, Mozambique, as well as countries that already include refugees in national life like Senegal. We look forward to learning more about other best practices shared today.
The United States will continue its effort to help bring an end to conflicts and persecution that cause forced displacement. We reiterate the importance of burden-sharing in refugee response and underscore that the costs of response will not continue to fall inequitably on the American taxpayer.
Thank you.
