Joan Atherton
USAID Senior Adviser
New York, New York
December 4, 2024
Remarks at the U.S. Intervention on International Development Cooperation at the Second Preparatory Committee Meeting for the 4th UN Financing for Development Conference
AS DELIVERED
Delivering on the SDGs and tackling global challenges like climate change, pandemics, fragility, and conflict is integral to poverty reduction and sustainable, inclusive, resilient growth and requires more financing than what public financing can do alone.
This is why the United States has led a global coalition to evolve the multilateral development banks to be better equipped to address today’s global challenges at scale.
A successful FfD4 outcome document will recognize and build on the work to reform and strengthen the MDBs and the international financial architecture and will focus on how to promote greater financing for development from all sources – public and private, domestic and international.
The United States is the largest or one of the largest shareholders in five of the largest MDBs and the largest donor to the International Development Association.
We proudly stand as the largest provider of official development assistance (ODA). The United States has not committed to the UN target of 0.7 percent of GNI to ODA.
We urge more reflection on the country programmable aid (CPA) proposal. Elements such as humanitarian assistance and debt relief are not included in ODA CPA. To commit to a rising share of CPA in ODA would have the effect of reducing humanitarian aid and debt relief to individual partner countries.
The United States strongly supports recognition of TOSSD alongside ODA. Harmonization of concepts and standards related to SDG 17.3.1, TOSSD and ODA was accomplished in the UN Statistical Commission’s and General Assembly’s adoption of the new SDG 17.3.1 indicator. We do not see the purpose of relitigating these relatively recent adoptions as an outcome for FfD4.
We strongly support efforts to simplify access to and increase the impact of climate finance and the steps the climate funds are taking to work together better as a system.
Effective multilateral collaboration requires respect for institutional governance, authorities and mandates, and for each partner to focus on its comparative advantage. For example, as the number of countries impacted by fragility, conflict, and violence grows, the UN’s role in addressing such issues is critical to help shape the enabling environment for IFI funding to continue to be effective and to attract greater private sector investment.
Decisions about MDB resources, financing volumes and terms, and strategic objectives are the purview of their respective Executive Boards, whose independence is critical to the financial sustainability and effectiveness of these institutions.
FfD4 should preserve the consensus that the most concessional resources should be focused on those with the greatest needs and least ability to mobilize other resources. MDB concessional resources are intended for the poorest countries, but can also be appropriately deployed in very limited instances as targeted incentives for middle income countries to pursue activities that address global challenges.
