John Kelley
Acting U.S. Alternate Representative
New York, New York
April 22, 2025
AS DELIVERED
Merci Beaucoup, Monsieur le Président.
Let me also thank SRSG Massieu and Ms. Casas for their presentations.
The United States will continue efforts to support a durable and lasting peace in Colombia.
In 2016, the United States supported implementation of Colombia’s Peace Accords and the UN Verification Mission in Colombia.
In the eight years since the Final Agreement, implementation of the peace agreement and its ability to address Colombia’s deteriorating security conditions remains a work in progress. While there has been advancement in some areas of the agreement, structural issues remain unaddressed and threaten to undermine progress made to date, and in the long run, sustainable peace for all Colombians.
There has been a significant escalation of violence from illegal armed groups since the beginning of this year. Violence continues to affect many departments in Colombia. In northern Colombia, in the Catatumbo region alone, it has resulted in over 100 deaths and the displacement of more than 64,000 people since January 16.
The level of attacks against former combatants increased, with 18 former combatants killed, from December 2024 to March 2025, up from five in the previous three months. The United States remains concerned about the potential spillover of violence into other parts of the country.
Women and children continue to remain disproportionately at risk as a result of increasing violence. The United States remains committed to protection of women and children in this conflict and condemns the recruitment and use of children by armed groups.
The “Total Peace” policy has yet to yield measurable results. Armed groups are splintering and misusing talks as an opportunity to expand their control and increase coca production. The limited presence of the state in northern areas continues to hinder its ability to reduce violence and illicit drug trade.
Illicit economies continue to fuel coca production, exacerbating patterns of crime. It is imperative that we intensify our efforts to expand economic opportunities for communities and reduce their dependence on illicit crops.
The United States remains very cautious about the Colombian government’s approach in negotiations with U.S.-designated foreign terrorist organizations and transnational criminal organizations. These entities have not yet demonstrated a genuine commitment to abandoning their illicit activities and seeking lasting peace. It is essential that these illicit armed groups show sincere intentions and concrete actions toward achieving peace.
Transitional justice mechanisms are important processes but need to progress with greater urgency. Past peace processes have made clear that having a security strategy is essential to pressure illegal armed groups to negotiate in good faith and to avoid impunity for their abuses. The United States is committed to preventing impunity for criminal acts by terrorists and other illegal armed groups.
Eight years into implementation of the Peace Accords, it is clear more work needs to be done. The United States will continue to monitor closely the process and effectiveness of the accords and work with members of this Council and the UN Verification Mission to achieve lasting peace and security in Colombia.
Je vous remercie, Monsieur le Président.
