UNITED STATES MISSION TO THE UNITED NATIONS
Office of Press and Public Diplomacy
April 21, 2025
Remarks on Item 3: Discussion on the theme “Implementing the United Nations Declaration on the Rights of Indigenous Peoples within United Nations Member States and the United Nations system, including identifying good practices and addressing challenges”
AS PREPARED
Thank you, Chair. Only statements made in our national capacity will be attributable to the United States.
From the beginning, the Trump Administration has been committed to fostering self-determination and expanding opportunities for Tribal nations.
Just a few weeks ago, the Administration completed a transfer of land to be held in trust for the Spirit Lake Nation. This was a significant step toward strengthening Tribal sovereignty and empowering the Spirit Lake Nation to use its trust lands for economic growth and community well-being.
In March, the U.S. Departments of State, Interior, and Homeland Security coordinated the successful return of seven sacred and ceremonial items from the Dutch state collections to the Ysleta del Sur Pueblo, a federally recognized Tribe in Texas.
By reconnecting Tribes and the Native Hawaiian Community with their cultural items, the United States Government is facilitating their freedoms of expression and religion and ensuring their heritage is appropriately honored and preserved. The return of these items has enabled the Tribe to perform religious ceremonies that have been interrupted for over 140 years.
All of this reflects the significant efforts of Indigenous persons, as well as the Administration’s commitment to strengthening Tribal sovereignty, promoting economic opportunities, and enhancing the quality of life for Indigenous Peoples.
The United States encourages other states to take similar steps and to align with the principles of the UN Declaration on the Rights of Indigenous Peoples (UNDRIP).
In our support of UNDRIP, the United States considers the concept of self-determination to be consistent with our existing recognition of, and relationship with, federally recognized tribes. We also understand free, prior, and informed consent to call for a process of meaningful consultation with tribal leaders, but not necessarily the agreement of those leaders, before the actions addressed in those consultations are taken.
I thank you.
