John Kelley
Acting U.S. Alternate Representative
New York, New York
April 7, 2025
AS DELIVERED
Merci Monsieur le Président. Let me also thank Under-Secretary-General Lacroix, Lieutenant General Saenz, and Lieutenant General Gomes for your presentations today.
Owing to their impartiality, military capabilities, and role in advancing political solutions, UN peacekeeping missions are well positioned to contribute to the success of ceasefires, enhancing efforts to create space for dialogue, foster trust between political actors, and more importantly, spare civilians from further violence.
Fundamentally, ceasefires must be respected by the conflict parties, including proxies and aligned forces, and reinforced by political processes in order for UN peacekeeping missions to be successful and for countries to achieve lasting peace.
We reject attacks on the credibility of UN peacekeeping missions such as those we have seen in recent months against MONUSCO by Rwandan officials and troops. Such attacks undermine trust in the findings of ceasefire observation missions and cast doubt on their impartiality.
The intelligence, surveillance, and reconnaissance capabilities of many of today’s missions allow for improved detection of ceasefire violations. Advancements in artificial intelligence may serve as a tool that assists missions to predict and pre-empt violations. To take full advantage of these capabilities, host countries and conflict parties must facilitate full freedom of movement for peacekeeping missions, including unmanned aerial vehicles and personnel.
Furthermore, while reporting by UN peacekeeping missions to this Council equips Member States with much-needed visibility on ceasefire violations, reporting must be more timely and prescriptive so swift accountability is possible.
As we have seen in both DRC and Lebanon, UN peacekeeping missions can serve as force multipliers to regional initiatives charged with monitoring ceasefires, including through the provision of logistical platforms and intelligence support. Such partnerships are most effective when they leverage the comparative advantages of each mechanism and when UN peacekeeping missions are empowered through broader peace processes.
In Lebanon, UNIFIL hosts the enhanced pentalateral mechanism, chaired by the United States with French participation, that is charged with monitoring all elements of the Cessation of Hostilities arrangement between Israel and Lebanon. Since it took effect on November 27, 2024, the level of violence has dramatically decreased and the Mechanism has facilitated, with support from UNIFIL, the deployment of Lebanese Armed Forces to approximately 100 positions south of the Litani River, compared to an estimated 10 positions at the beginning of the Cessation of Hostilities.
In the DRC, we once again call for an immediate ceasefire and halt to M23 and Rwanda’s advances in North and South Kivu. We support a role for MONUSCO in overseeing such a ceasefire in close partnership with regional initiatives, as appropriate.
Mr. President, the challenges we currently face in the DRC and Lebanon underscore the necessity for UN peacekeeping to be more efficient, adaptable, and focused on long-term political solutions to achieve lasting peace and security.
Je vous remercie Monsieur le Président.
