John Kelley
Acting U.S. Alternate Representative
New York, New York
April 04, 2025
AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Ambassador, and thank you to Sierra Leone and the A3+ for convening this important meeting on small arms control and weapons management in UN sanctions regimes.
We’re all aware of the detrimental effect diversion and illicit trafficking in arms, including small arms and light weapons, have on international peace and security.
The challenge facing us is how to bolster implementation.
Blatant violations of arms embargoes by Member States and prohibited conduct by other actors have largely gone unchecked and without consequence.
We strongly support enforcement mechanisms like the Libya vessel inspections mandate, a critical deterrent to would-be arms and oil smugglers. We urge the Council to renew this mandate in May. We would similarly call for action to strengthen the UN Verification and Inspection Mechanism for Yemen, to detect and deter the illicit flow of arms to the Houthis in Yemen.
We urge fellow Council members to consider the impact obstruction of sanctions expert panels, including delays in appointments, has on our collective ability to assess and address implementation gaps.
We further call on Council members to employ targeted sanctions designations to promote accountability for those implicated in activities prohibited by UN arms embargoes.
The Security Council meets frequently to negotiate sanctions regimes and peacekeeping mandates designed to improve weapons stockpile security, prevent illicit cross-border arms flows, combat non-state actors that seek to circumvent sanctions regimes, and support disarmament programs.
In those discussions, our position has been clear: arms embargoes – and Panel mandates monitoring their implementation – serve as a critical deterrent to stem the flow of illicit weapons, decrease the capacity of combatants to engage in conflict, and ultimately protect civilians.
Concerningly, however, we have seen a concerted effort by some Member States to weaken or abolish arms embargoes and peacekeeping mandates absent real progress on security sector reforms and peaceful political transitions.
Looking ahead, we believe the Informal Working Group on Sanctions should develop practical recommendations to strengthen arms embargos and improve implementation of sanctions generally. We look forward to those discussions as soon as a chair is appointed.
And with that I thank you, Mr. Ambassador.
