April 2, 2025
AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Mr. President, and thank you High Commissioner Turk, for your meaningful reporting and briefing on missing persons.
The United States has no higher priority than keeping its citizens safe, and this commitment drives the relentless efforts to locate and assist Americans missing overseas. President Trump has made the return of American citizens a bedrock priority of his Administration. We strongly support addressing the persistent and alarming issue of missing persons during and after conflict. Around the world, families have to live with unbearable uncertainty, unable to mourn, to grieve, and to honor their missing loved ones. Every one of them deserves closure.
We would like to take this opportunity to highlight several countries of concern regarding missing persons.
In Afghanistan, since 2021, the Taliban have been responsible for numerous missing persons, including the disappearances of human rights defenders, legal professionals, and protesters, among others.
We urge the Government of Turkmenistan to release verifiable information on the whereabouts and conditions of disappeared persons, including to family members who have been unable to ascertain the status of their loved ones.
We welcome efforts from countries to investigate and seek justice for missing persons.
In Syria, after 14 years of civil war and brutal authoritarian rule, there remain countless missing persons. Peace and stability in Syria will depend on effectively ascertaining the fate of the missing, including Americans like Austin Tice, and advancing justice and accountability. We urge all parties to cooperate with the Independent Institution on Missing Persons in Syria, the International, Impartial and Independent Mechanism, and Syrian-led accountability efforts.
In Bangladesh, the Interim Government established a commission of inquiry on enforced disappearances, and a number of victims have been released from secret detention facilities over the past several months.
We are troubled by continued reports of enforced disappearances perpetrated by cartels in Mexico, and welcome Mexico’s efforts on accountability and forensic identification of missing persons. We will continue to work with government authorities at all levels and civil society in Mexico to address these systemic and pervasive challenges that are perpetrated by cartels.
Mr. High Commissioner, I would lie to pose a question. What steps can the international community take to help build the technical capacity of countries to find missing persons?
Thank you.
